WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.225
Book Two, Proposition Four.

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:11.921
If a segment be cut at random, the square on the whole is equal to the squares on the segments and twice the rectangle contained by the segments.

3
00:00:11.921 --> 00:00:14.730
Let the segment A B be cut at random at C.

4
00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:22.574
On A B, describe the square A D E B by One Forty-Six, and join the diagonal B D.

5
00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:34.360
Through C, draw C F parallel to A D or B E by One Thirty-One, meeting B D at G; and through G, draw H K parallel to A B or D E.

6
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:42.780
Because C F is parallel to A D, the exterior angle C G B equals the interior angle A D B by One Twenty-Nine.

7
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.892
And since A B equals A D, angle A D B equals angle A B D by One Five.

8
00:00:47.892 --> 00:00:57.966
Therefore angle C G B equals angle G B C, so side B C equals C G by One Six, and by One Thirty-Four all four sides of C G K B are equal.

9
00:00:57.966 --> 00:01:06.041
Moreover, since C G is parallel to B K and angle K B C is right, all four angles of C G K B are right angles.

10
00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:13.597
Thus C G K B is the square on B C, and for the same reason H F is the square on H G, which equals A C.

11
00:01:13.597 --> 00:01:25.523
Now the complements A G and G E are equal, and since C G equals B C, each is the rectangle contained by A C and B C, so together they equal twice that rectangle.

12
00:01:25.523 --> 00:01:31.745
Adding the two squares H F and C K, the four areas compose the whole square A D E B.

13
00:01:31.745 --> 00:01:40.116
Therefore, the square on A B equals the squares on A C and B C plus twice the rectangle contained by A C and B C.

14
00:01:40.116 --> 00:01:41.820
Which was to be proved.

15
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:51.050
Proposition Two Four is the geometric archetype of the binomial square: the square of a plus b equals a squared plus b squared plus two a b.

16
00:01:51.050 --> 00:02:00.679
Rather than multiplying symbols, Euclid dissects the square along its diagonal into two squares about the diameter and two equal rectangular complements.

17
00:02:00.679 --> 00:02:16.600
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G eighty-five unites eleven prerequisites across forty-eight ancestor nodes—combining isosceles triangle symmetry, parallel transversals, and parallelogram complements into a cornerstone of Book Ten's theory of irrationals.

18
